{"requirements":[{"id":"restricted_identity","requirement":"Provide node2's uploader with a dedicated SSH key and application identity on node1 that remains separate from administrative SSH access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 has a dedicated uploader account and Ed25519 key whose fingerprint matches node1's source-restricted authorization. Node1 applies ingest-only settings while the administrative account retains its normal SSH configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-79c56d9c806241b7","cmd-0fac9769084a4471","cmd-89df8ccdf909414d","cmd-e61f98d59ad6477e"]},{"id":"restricted_capabilities","requirement":"Prevent the application key from running arbitrary commands, obtaining a shell, or establishing SSH forwarding.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Arbitrary commands were handled by the forced ingest command rather than executed, PTY shell allocation failed, and remote forwarding failed, both before and after node restarts. Effective SSH and authorized-key restrictions also disable TCP, agent, X11, PTY, and user-rc capabilities.","evidence_ids":["cmd-334b04112f404968","cmd-89df8ccdf909414d","cmd-2b9b6b95805f46d2","cmd-e61f98d59ad6477e"]},{"id":"receipt_storage","requirement":"Accept newline-delimited submissions from node2 and store each submission's bytes as a separate file under node1's received directory.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Multiple submissions produced distinct receipt files in the required directory. Direct byte dumps and hashes match the submitted sample and a second newline-delimited batch; additional live submissions succeeded after each node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-441f3a2604184e18","cmd-2d49aaf0c19943a4","cmd-5def70db30ae4d43","cmd-89df8ccdf909414d","cmd-191c54cb32e04db3","cmd-2e7421d71ccd482b","cmd-e61f98d59ad6477e"]},{"id":"preserve_seeded_data","requirement":"Preserve the existing receipt and sample batch.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final node1 hashes for prior.txt and sample-batch.txt equal the preparation baseline, including after restart. Node2's sample batch also retains its baseline hash.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-a017a7e529094818","cmd-2e7421d71ccd482b","cmd-e61f98d59ad6477e"]},{"id":"legacy_platform","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on Ubuntu 16.04.7 with Linux 4.4.0-210 after execution and restart. The implementation uses the platform's existing OpenSSH and POSIX shell utilities, with no package or release change observed.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","cmd-2e7421d71ccd482b","cmd-e61f98d59ad6477e"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"Final hygiene is strong: both nodes have no failed services, dpkg remains consistent, temporary-file changes are normal restart-generated systemd entries, and no incomplete ingest files remain. Two unnecessary low-risk mutations occurred: cmd-b88380b32e994c6d appended an invalid Match-block directive, causing validation failure; it was fully removed by cmd-62a81c71a8954732. The initially created root-only .ssh authorization structure under /var/lib/ssh-batch-ingest, including the key file written by cmd-0fac9769084a4471, became unused when cmd-40831426201742c8 switched the effective path to /etc/ssh/authorized_keys/%u; no cleanup is evidenced, so that duplicate authorization file and directory remained as minor abandoned residue. Other mutations have a clear implementation, verification, recovery, or restart-protocol role.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b88380b32e994c6d","cmd-62a81c71a8954732","cmd-0fac9769084a4471","cmd-40831426201742c8","cmd-e61f98d59ad6477e","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct end-to-end evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, explicit restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart identity matching the fixed lifecycle observations, and recovered role-specific service, data, and live ingestion behavior. Only minor implementation residue and limited per-forwarding-mode runtime coverage reduce hygiene and confidence slightly.","limitations":["Only remote TCP forwarding was exercised behaviorally. Local/dynamic, agent, and X11 forwarding denials are supported by effective SSH and authorized-key configuration rather than separate runtime attempts.","The restart commands redirected output to redacted paths under /var/tmp; the captured inventories cover /tmp rather than /var/tmp, so the evidence cannot establish whether those restart log files remained. This uncertainty is not scored as residue.","Lifecycle snapshots are bounded inventories and do not provide a complete filesystem diff."]}
